put.1992.0.B6.reviewed_statement:
Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a set of real $n \times n$ matrices such that

(i)  $I \in \mathcal{M}$, where $I$ is the $n \times n$ identity matrix;

(ii)  if $A \in \mathcal{M}$ and $B \in \mathcal{M}$, then either $AB \in \mathcal{M}$ or
$-AB \in \mathcal{M}$, but not both;

(iii)  if $A \in \mathcal{M}$ and $B \in \mathcal{M}$, then either $AB = BA$ or
$AB = -BA$;

(iv)  if $A \in \mathcal{M}$ and $A \neq I$, there is at least one $B \in
\mathcal{M}$ such that $AB = - BA$.

Prove that $\mathcal{M}$ contains at most $n^2$ matrices.

